# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored and must never be committed.
#
# This file IS committed — it is the contract that tells the next person (and you in three
# months) which variables exist. Add a key to env.validation.ts and to this file in the same
# commit, or the two drift silently.
#
# In production neither file exists: values come from the platform's secret store.

NODE_ENV=development
# Backend on 3001, frontend on 3000.
PORT=3001

# ── Database — runs natively on this machine, NOT in Docker (TR-DEC-015) ──────
# PostgreSQL 18, managed with pgAdmin, connecting as the `postgres` superuser.
#
# One-time setup: create the database in pgAdmin (right-click Databases → Create →
# Database → name it `ticketrush`). TypeORM creates TABLES via migrations but cannot create
# the DATABASE itself — it has to connect to something that already exists.
#
# Known tradeoff (TR-DEC-015): the app connects with superuser privileges, so a SQL
# injection or a bad migration can reach every database on the server, not just this one.
# Accepted for local development; flagged for the M8 security review.
DATABASE_HOST=localhost
DATABASE_PORT=5432
DATABASE_USER=postgres
DATABASE_PASSWORD=
DATABASE_NAME=ticketrush

# ── Redis — Docker (backend/docker-compose.yml) ───────────────────────────────
REDIS_HOST=localhost
REDIS_PORT=6379

# ── RabbitMQ — Docker ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not connected to until M6; validated from M0 so a typo surfaces at boot rather than three
# weeks later inside a consumer nobody is watching. Management UI: http://localhost:15672
RABBITMQ_USER=ticketrush
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=ticketrush
RABBITMQ_URL=amqp://ticketrush:ticketrush@localhost:5672

# ── HTTP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Explicit origin allow-list. Never a wildcard combined with credentials.
# This is the FRONTEND's origin (Next.js on 3000), not this server's.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# ── Auth (M1) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signs the access token. Generate a real one — 32 bytes of entropy, minimum:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# This is NOT the frontend's NEXTAUTH_SECRET. Two secrets, two systems: this one signs the
# access token NestJS issues, that one encrypts NextAuth's session cookie. Keeping them
# separate is deliberate — leaking NEXTAUTH_SECRET lets an attacker forge a convincing
# session cookie but not a valid access token, so the API still rejects them.
#
# A short secret is brute-forceable offline: anyone holding one token from this system can
# grind candidate secrets until a signature verifies, then mint tokens for any user.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=

# Short by design. This value IS the blast radius of an XSS token theft (TR-DEC-002) — the
# access token is readable by client JS, so its lifetime bounds how long a stolen one works.
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN=15m

# Refresh tokens are opaque random values, not JWTs, stored as SHA-256 hashes — so there is
# no refresh signing secret, only a lifetime. A fast hash is correct for an already
# high-entropy value; slow hashes are for guessable secrets like passwords.
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_DAYS=7

# TR-DEC-017. Seconds an already-rotated refresh token is still accepted rather than treated
# as theft. Fixes the lost-response race and NextAuth's parallel-refresh race with one
# mechanism. Short on purpose: within this window a stolen token still works.
REFRESH_GRACE_SECONDS=30

# bcrypt cost factor (TR-DEC-016). 12 ≈ 250ms on current hardware — the standard
# 200–500ms budget. Lower it in tests; hashing at 12 across a suite that creates users
# costs minutes of CPU.
BCRYPT_COST=12

# ── Stripe (M5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full walkthrough: docs/guides/stripe-test-setup.md. Test mode only — env.validation.ts
# rejects anything not starting sk_test_/whsec_ at boot, before a live key could ever be used.
#
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY    — Dashboard → Developers → API keys → Secret key (test mode toggle ON).
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET — printed by `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3001/api/webhooks/stripe`
#                         in development; a real deployed endpoint gets its own from the Dashboard.
# STRIPE_SUCCESS_URL / STRIPE_CANCEL_URL — the FRONTEND redirects Checkout sends the browser to.
#   Never trusted as proof of payment — the webhook alone decides that.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
STRIPE_SUCCESS_URL=http://localhost:3000/checkout/success
STRIPE_CANCEL_URL=http://localhost:3000/checkout/cancel

# ── Added per module, not up front ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each is added to env.validation.ts in the module that first needs it, so a missing key
# fails the boot of the build that actually requires it.
